{"name":"Viaduct","hex":"#c1bbb1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"viaduct","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bridge with several spans that carries rail or road traffic over a valley or other obstacles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/viaduct"}],"entries":[{"title":"Viaduct","blocks":[{"text":"A viaduct is a bridge that consists of a series of arches, piers or columns supporting a long elevated railway or road. Typically a viaduct connects two points of roughly equal elevation, allowing direct overpass across a wide valley, road, river, or other low-lying terrain features and obstacles. The term viaduct is derived from the Latin via meaning \"road\", and ducere meaning \"to lead\". It is a 19th-century derivation from an analogy with ancient Roman aqueducts. Like the Roman aqueducts, many early viaducts comprised a series of arches of roughly equal length."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Laighmilton1.JPG/330px-Laighmilton1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viaduct","wikidata":"Q181348"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Ouse_Valley_Viaduct_02.jpg/500px-Ouse_Valley_Viaduct_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Ouse_Valley_Viaduct_02.jpg/1280px-Ouse_Valley_Viaduct_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Ouse_Valley_Viaduct_02.jpg/500px-Ouse_Valley_Viaduct_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Ouse Valley Viaduct in Sussex, England","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOuse_Valley_Viaduct_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Viaduct_near_Slade%2C_Plymouth_-_Ni